LESSONS_P1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Supplier record: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module covers the Supplier master and the buying foundations in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: create Supplier records with the full field set including the compliance fields; design the Supplier Group tree; maintain supplier contacts, addresses, and bank details under the change controls; configure currencies and payment terms with the document default chain; operate the payables posture; run the supplier lifecycle from onboarding through hold to retirement; and run the supplier data reads on their cadences.</p><p><b>What a Supplier record is.</b> A Supplier is the register record of a party the business buys from. Every purchasing document — Material Request routing, Request for Quotation, Purchase Order, Purchase Receipt, Purchase Invoice — names a supplier, and pulls its behaviour from that supplier's record: the currency the supplier bills in, the payment terms the business has agreed, the addresses goods ship from and invoices come from, the tax treatment, and the bank account the business pays into. The Supplier record is the buying side's mirror of the Customer record, and the same construction rule applies: documents behave correctly because the master was built correctly.</p><p><b>What reads the Supplier record.</b></p><p>1. <b>The purchasing documents</b> — every transaction in modules 3-7 begins by naming the supplier.<br>2. <b>The payables ledger</b> — supplier balances, due dates, and the payment schedule all key on the record.<br>3. <b>The costing machinery</b> — module 2's buying price lists and the landed-cost flow resolve through the supplier.<br>4. <b>The compliance machinery</b> — withholding tax applicability, TIN records, and the documentation the statutory returns require.<br>5. <b>The payment run</b> — the bank details on the record are where the business's money goes; for this reason those fields carry the tightest change control in the register (Chapter 4).</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The procurement function operates the records; the owner decides terms postures per the ladder; the accountant owns the payables side, the tax fields, and the withholding rulings; bank-detail changes run the verification procedure without exception; and record creation runs through the onboarding gate (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>Build order.</b> The tree before the records, the records before the documents: the Supplier Group tree designed (Chapter 3); the onboarding gate written (Chapter 7); suppliers created through the gate with contacts, addresses, and verified bank details (Chapters 2, 4); currencies and terms set per the decisions (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Supplier doctype walk. Chapter 3: the Supplier Group tree. Chapter 4: contacts, addresses, and bank details. Chapter 5: currencies, terms, and the default chain. Chapter 6: the payables posture. Chapter 7: the supplier lifecycle and the data reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Supplier doctype", "12", "<p>Open Supplier (awesome bar) &gt; New. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Supplier Name.</b> The registered legal name per the naming convention — the name on the supplier's own invoices and registration documents, consistently formatted. The name prints on Purchase Orders and appears on every payables report; it is entered once, from documents, at the gate.</p><p><b>2. Supplier Group.</b> The classification from Chapter 3's tree — pharmaceutical manufacturers, importers and trading houses, equipment vendors, service providers. The group is a working field: buying defaults can derive from it, the spend reports aggregate by it, and the approval postures the policy sets can reference it.</p><p><b>3. Supplier Type and Country.</b> Company or Individual, and the supplier's country. The country field matters operationally: it signals the currency and import questions Chapter 5 covers, and it drives which compliance documentation the gate requires — a domestic manufacturer and a foreign manufacturer carry different verification sets.</p><p><b>4. Billing Currency.</b> The currency the supplier invoices in. This field sets the currency of every document raised on the supplier, and it is a structural decision: a supplier billing in a foreign currency exposes the business to exchange movements between order and payment, which the terms decision (Chapter 5) must account for.</p><p><b>5. Default Price List (buying).</b> The buying price list the supplier's documents pre-fill from — module 2 covers the buying lists. The resolution chain mirrors the selling side: the supplier's own default, else the group's, else the buying default.</p><p><b>6. Payment Terms Template.</b> The terms the business has agreed with this supplier — the template that computes due dates on every Purchase Invoice (Chapter 6). Set from the negotiated agreement, not assumed.</p><p><b>7. Tax and compliance fields.</b> Per the accountant's ruling table:</p><p>1. <b>The supplier's TIN</b> — required for the statutory documentation of purchases and the withholding-tax records.<br>2. <b>Withholding tax applicability</b> — whether payments to this supplier attract withholding under the rules the accountant maintains (service suppliers commonly do; goods suppliers per the ruling), and at which category. The field carries the accountant's ruling; the payment machinery applies it; the operator neither decides nor overrides it.<br>3. <b>Tax category</b> where the site uses category-based purchase tax rules.</p><p><b>8. Hold and Disabled.</b> Two distinct instruments: <b>Hold</b> suspends new business with a live supplier (quality failures, disputes) while history and open items remain workable — the hold type controls what is blocked (invoices, payments, or all); <b>Disabled</b> retires the record at zero balance when the relationship ends. Suppliers are never deleted; document history references every supplier that ever traded.</p><p><b>Verification per record.</b> The name from documents; the group and country correct; the currency as the supplier actually bills; terms per the agreement; TIN and withholding per the ruling; the primary contact, address, and verified bank details attached (Chapter 4).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Supplier Group tree", "12", "<p>One tree classifies the supply base. This chapter is its design procedure.</p><p><b>1. What the tree answers.</b> The Supplier Group answers the commercial question: what kind of supply is this? The classes are the kinds of supply the business manages differently — different terms postures, different approval sensitivity, different reads. For a pharmaceutical distributor: manufacturers (the direct factory relationships), importers and trading houses (the intermediary channel), equipment vendors, logistics and transport, and service providers (professional and facility services). A class with no management difference is a report filter, not a group.</p><p><b>2. The design procedure.</b> On paper first, per the standing tree rules:</p><p>1. <b>List the supply classes the business actually treats differently.</b> The test questions: do these suppliers carry different payment postures? Different compliance requirements (withholding on services)? Different quality regimes (manufacturers carry product-quality obligations that service providers do not)? Different spend review?<br>2. <b>Three levels or fewer</b> — the root, the major classes, and subdivisions only where management genuinely differs (manufacturers split domestic and foreign where the terms and compliance sets differ, which for an importing distributor they do).<br>3. <b>Wire the defaults</b> — where a class shares a terms posture or price list, set it at the group so new members inherit, and record the wiring in the convention.</p><p><b>3. The classification's uses, stated.</b></p><p>1. <b>Spend analysis</b> — purchases by group is the first cut of every spend review: what the business spends on manufacturers versus intermediaries versus services is a structural figure the owner reads quarterly.<br>2. <b>Policy application</b> — the procurement policy's rules reference groups: which classes require competitive quotes (module 4), which carry the scorecard regime (module 8), which attract withholding review.<br>3. <b>Risk reading</b> — concentration by group (one manufacturer carrying most of a class's volume) is a supply-risk figure the quarterly review compiles.</p><p><b>4. The change discipline.</b> The tree changes by decision with the reason recorded; reclassifying a supplier is an ordinary edit with a note; restructuring is a governed event. The rule is identical to the selling side because the cost is identical: every new node fragments the history of the reports that aggregate on it.</p><p><b>5. The classification read — quarterly.</b> Suppliers per group sampled against the definitions; the importer that became a manufacturer's direct channel regrouped; near-empty groups with no management difference flagged for merge at the structural review. The read rides the same quarterly calendar as the rest of the programme's structure reviews.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Contacts, addresses, and bank details", "13", "<p>The people, places, and payment destinations of supply. The third of these carries the register's tightest control.</p><p><b>1. Contacts.</b> One Contact record per person, linked to the supplier:</p><p>1. <b>The sales contact</b> — the supplier's person who receives Purchase Orders and answers availability questions: the primary contact, because a Purchase Order sent to a generic address is an order nobody at the supplier owns.<br>2. <b>The accounts contact</b> — the supplier's receivables person: where remittance advices go and where statement reconciliations are conducted.<br>3. <b>The quality and escalation contacts</b> where the class requires them (manufacturers carry a quality contact for the module 6 receiving disputes and module 8 scorecard conversations).</p><p><b>2. Addresses.</b> One Address record per physical location, linked and flagged: the billing address (where the supplier invoices from — it prints on the compliance records) and the shipping origin addresses where the business arranges collection or tracks origin. Multi-site suppliers (a manufacturer despatching from two plants) carry an address per site, titled per the convention.</p><p><b>3. Bank details — the controlled fields.</b> The supplier's bank account is where the business's money goes, and supplier-bank-detail fraud — an emailed instruction to change the account, followed by payments to the fraudster — is among the most common and expensive frauds in payables. The register therefore treats these fields under a written control:</p><p>1. <b>Entry at the gate</b> — bank details enter from the supplier's official documentation and are verified by the call-back procedure: a call to the supplier's known number (the number from the register, never the number on the instruction) confirming the account name, number, and bank, with the verification recorded — who called, whom they spoke to, when.<br>2. <b>Changes run the same verification, without exception</b> — an emailed change instruction is precisely the fraud pattern; the change is not entered until the call-back to the known contact confirms it. Urgency in the instruction raises suspicion rather than lowering the control.<br>3. <b>Write permission</b> — bank fields are writable by the named roles only, per the matrix; every change is system-logged.<br>4. <b>The bank-change read — monthly</b> — every bank-detail change in the month, with who changed it and the verification record attached. A change without its verification is treated as a live incident: payments to the account stop until the verification completes.</p><p><b>4. Hygiene.</b> Quarterly, riding the standing calendar: primary contacts confirmed for the top suppliers by spend; addresses sampled against recent documents; leavers replaced. The payables function surfaces dead contacts naturally — a remittance advice that bounces is a hygiene finding routed to the read.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Currencies, terms, and the default chain", "12", "<p>Three master fields — currency, payment terms, price list — flow from the Supplier record onto every purchasing document. This chapter is the chain and the two decisions riding it.</p><p><b>1. The default chain.</b> At document creation, the supplier's fields pre-fill the document: the billing currency sets the document currency; the payment terms template sets the schedule the invoice will compute due dates from; the price list supplies the expected rates (module 2). The chain is wired once, verified at the gate's first-document check, and the operator's job at document time is verification, not composition — the same rule as the selling side.</p><p><b>2. Foreign-currency suppliers.</b> A supplier billing in a foreign currency introduces exchange exposure: the naira cost of the commitment moves between order, receipt, and payment as the rate moves. The operational rules:</p><p>1. <b>The document carries the supplier's currency and the exchange rate</b> at posting, per the rate source the Accounts track configures; the ledger carries both the foreign amount and the naira value.<br>2. <b>The exposure is priced at commitment</b> — the module 4 order decision accounts for the exposure window (order to payment), and the terms decision below interacts with it: longer terms on a foreign-currency supplier lengthen the exposure window.<br>3. <b>Revaluation and settlement differences are the accountant's territory</b> — the Accounts track's period procedures; the procurement obligation is documents with honest dates and correct currencies so those procedures compute from true records.</p><p><b>3. The terms decision.</b> Payment terms with a supplier are a negotiated asset: <b>30-day terms</b> mean the business holds its cash thirty days longer per purchase cycle, and the working-capital value of that holding is real and computable. The postures:</p><p>1. <b>Terms are negotiated at onboarding and renegotiated at review</b> — per the ladder: the standard ask per class, the owner's decision on strategic relationships.<br>2. <b>Terms are recorded as templates</b> (Chapter 2) — the template computes every invoice's due date; terms that exist only in conversation compute nothing.<br>3. <b>Early-payment discounts are evaluated, not reflexively taken or ignored</b> — a discount for early settlement is priced against the working-capital cost per the accountant's arithmetic, and the decision per supplier is recorded.</p><p><b>4. The chain read — quarterly.</b> A sample per supplier class: a draft document per supplier confirming the currency, terms, and list pre-fill as the register states; discrepancies corrected at the register. The read is cheap and it is the difference between a register that describes the supply base and one that merely lists it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The payables posture", "12", "<p>Payables are managed deliberately, as receivables are on the selling side. This chapter is the posture — the decisions and reads that govern what the business owes.</p><p><b>1. The payables position.</b> The Accounts Payable report: open Purchase Invoices per supplier, bucketed by age against due date. The figures depend on the disciplines this track builds: invoices posted from documents (module 7), due dates from templates (Chapter 5), payments allocated per remittances. The position is read weekly by the payables function and monthly by the owner with the payables summary.</p><p><b>2. Paying on terms.</b> The standing posture is stated plainly: <b>the business pays on the due date — neither habitually early nor habitually late.</b></p><p>1. <b>Not habitually early</b>, because terms were negotiated for their working-capital value and paying early returns that value unasked; early payment is a decision (a priced discount, a strategic relationship gesture), not a default.<br>2. <b>Not habitually late</b>, because lateness is priced by suppliers into the next negotiation, surfaces as supply risk (the manufacturer that ships the reliable payer first), and on strategic relationships costs more than the cash timing gains. The scorecard conversation (module 8) runs in both directions; suppliers score their customers too, informally and consequentially.</p><p><b>3. The payment run.</b> Payments run on a cadence (weekly per the policy), from the due list:</p><p>1. <b>The run's list</b> — invoices due in the window, verified (three-way matched per module 7), with withholding computed per the rulings.<br>2. <b>The approvals</b> — the run is approved per the matrix's bands; individual off-run payments are exceptions with named approval.<br>3. <b>The execution</b> — payments to the register's verified bank details only (Chapter 4's control closing its loop here); remittance advices to the supplier's accounts contact.</p><p><b>4. Supplier statement reconciliation.</b> The mirror of the selling side's statement cycle, run from the receiving end: the supplier's statement against the business's ledger, monthly for the top suppliers by spend, differences traced through the document references — the missing invoice, the unallocated payment, the disputed receipt — and resolved at the document that differs. A supplier account that reconciles monthly never produces the year-end investigation backlog of one that does not; the read's cost is minutes per account.</p><p><b>5. The payables summary — monthly.</b> The owner's page: total payables, due profile for the coming weeks (the cash requirement), overdue value with reasons, withholding remitted and pending, and the terms map — average terms held per class against the policy's targets. One page, filed with the monthly review.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The supplier lifecycle and the data reads", "13", "<p>Suppliers are vetted in, held when trouble surfaces, and retired when relationships end. This chapter is the lifecycle and the standing reads.</p><p><b>1. The onboarding gate.</b> Suppliers enter the register by gate, in order:</p><p>1. <b>The duplicate check first</b> — the register searched by name fragments, TIN, phone, and bank account before creation. A duplicate supplier splits spend history and payables across records and defeats the concentration reads.<br>2. <b>Verification against documents</b> — the registration documents (CAC registration for Nigerian companies), the TIN, and the trading address verified against official documentation; for foreign suppliers, the equivalent registration set per the compliance owner's checklist. The verification protects against both error and the fictitious-supplier fraud pattern.<br>3. <b>Bank details by the call-back procedure</b> (Chapter 4) — entered from official documentation, verified to the known number, recorded.<br>4. <b>Classification</b> — group per the tree's definitions.<br>5. <b>Terms and currency per the decisions</b> — the negotiated terms as a template; the billing currency confirmed.<br>6. <b>Compliance fields per the ruling</b> — TIN recorded; withholding applicability ruled by the accountant.<br>7. <b>The first-document check</b> — a draft Purchase Order confirming the pre-fill chain; deleted after reading.</p><p><b>2. Hold.</b> The instrument for live trouble: quality failures under investigation, unresolved disputes, a failed verification. The hold enters with its reason and its type (blocking new orders, invoices, payments, or all per the case), the supplier is informed where the policy says so, and the hold is reviewed on a stated date — a hold without a review date becomes a forgotten relationship rather than a managed one. Release is a decision recorded with what resolved.</p><p><b>3. Retirement.</b> At relationship end: open items settled to zero, the record Disabled with the reason, the history preserved. The annual pass sweeps dormant suppliers (no documents across the policy window) into the retirement decision — a dormant record with live bank details and open permissions is exposure with no relationship attached.</p><p><b>4. The supplier data reads, consolidated.</b></p><p>1. <b>The bank-change read — monthly</b> (Chapter 4): every change against its verification; unverified changes treated as incidents.<br>2. <b>The duplicate-candidate read — monthly:</b> near-identical names, shared TINs or bank accounts across records — a shared bank account across two supplier records is a fraud indicator investigated, not merely merged.<br>3. <b>The classification read — quarterly</b> (Chapter 3).<br>4. <b>The chain read — quarterly</b> (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>The hygiene read — quarterly</b> (Chapter 4).<br>6. <b>The dormancy read — quarterly:</b> candidates for retirement; holds past their review dates.<br>7. <b>The concentration read — quarterly:</b> spend share by supplier within each group — the single-source classes and the one-supplier dependencies listed for the owner's risk review, with the figures and the suppliers named.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: HealthTrade's supply base", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to the standing case: the supply base behind the selling and inventory operations the other tracks built. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>The base.</b> HealthTrade's purchasing concentrates on: <b>two local pharmaceutical manufacturers</b> — Emzor and a second domestic manufacturer — supplying the core pharmaceutical range in naira; <b>one Indian manufacturer</b> supplying selected generics, billing in US dollars; and a European equipment vendor for the retail-equipment line, billing in euros. The register therefore carries <b>three currencies</b>, and the base's design places the two channels the tree distinguishes — domestic manufacturers and foreign manufacturers — under different compliance sets.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the tree (Chapter 3).</b> The Supplier Group tree: Manufacturers (Domestic, Foreign), Importers &amp; Trading Houses, Equipment Vendors, Logistics, Services. Group defaults wire the domestic manufacturers' standard terms posture; the Services group carries the withholding flag per the accountant's ruling table.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the gate runs four times (Chapter 7).</b> Each supplier onboards through the full gate: duplicate checks clean; CAC and TIN verification for the domestic pair; the foreign registration sets for the Indian manufacturer and the equipment vendor per the compliance checklist; bank details entered from official documentation and verified by call-back to known numbers, each verification recorded. The first-document checks confirm the pre-fill chains.</p><p><b>Step 3 — currencies and terms (Chapter 5).</b> Emzor and the second domestic manufacturer bill in naira on <b>30-day terms</b> — negotiated at onboarding, recorded as the template, the working-capital value noted in the decision file. The Indian manufacturer bills in US dollars; the terms decision records the exposure window explicitly (order to payment across the import lead time), and the order sizing in module 5 will account for it. The equipment vendor bills in euros on the project-payment terms its class carries.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the payables posture installs (Chapter 6).</b> The weekly payment run stands up from the due list; the pay-on-terms posture is written into the policy; supplier statement reconciliation begins monthly with the two domestic manufacturers (the top of the spend concentration). The first monthly payables summary compiles: the due profile, the terms map, and a clean bank-change read.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the reads return their first findings (Chapter 7).</b> The duplicate-candidate read flags a legacy trading-house record with the same TIN as a retired importer — merged per the remediation with the documents re-pointed. The concentration read states the structural figure the owner already suspected: the domestic pharmaceutical class concentrates heavily on Emzor — recorded with the figures for the risk review, where the second manufacturer's range overlap becomes the mitigation conversation. The first quarter closes with the register verified, the chains confirmed, and every read filed.</p><p><b>The register evidence.</b> The gate files hold the verifications; the decision files hold the terms with their reasoning; the bank-change read holds its clean month; the concentration read holds the risk figure with names. The supply base is answerable from its files, per the standing standard, before a single Purchase Order ships — which is the state module 3's demand machinery builds on.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in supplier foundations, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the unverified bank change.</b> Symptom: bank details changed on an emailed instruction; payments to an account nobody verified; the fraud discovered at the supplier's overdue call. Correction: payments stopped, the incident procedure run, recovery attempted through the bank. Prevention: the call-back procedure without exception, write permission per the matrix, and the monthly bank-change read.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the duplicate supplier.</b> Symptom: one supplier's spend and balance split across records; concentration reads understated; two records sharing one bank account. Correction: investigated first (the shared account may be the fraud indicator), then merged per the remediation. Prevention: the gate's duplicate check across name, TIN, phone, and bank account.</p><p><b>Error 3 — terms that exist only in conversation.</b> Symptom: due dates improvised per invoice; the negotiated 30 days neither enforced nor measured; the working-capital value leaking. Correction: templates applied from the decision files. Prevention: every negotiated term recorded as a template at the gate.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the habitual posture.</b> Symptom: paying everything early (the negotiated value returned unasked) or everything late (supply risk and priced-in lateness). Correction: the payment run rebuilt on the due list; exceptions become decisions. Prevention: the pay-on-terms posture and the weekly run.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the unmanaged hold.</b> Symptom: suppliers held without review dates; relationships frozen by inattention; or trouble suppliers never held at all, with new orders flowing through open disputes. Correction: holds reviewed and resolved by decision. Prevention: every hold with a reason, a type, and a review date.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the fictitious supplier.</b> Symptom: a record created without document verification; invoices approved against no real supply; the classic payables fraud. Correction: the incident procedure, the record frozen, the approval chain examined. Prevention: the gate's document verification, the segregation the matrix enforces between record creation and payment approval, and the duplicate read's bank-account matching.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the Supplier walk including the compliance fields and the hold-versus-disabled distinction; design the tree with the management-difference test; operate contacts, addresses, and the bank-detail control with the call-back procedure; state the default chain and the foreign-currency rules; run the payables posture — pay on terms, the run, statement reconciliation; run the gate, hold, and retirement; and run the seven data reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 1: every purchasing document stands on its Supplier record — created through the gate with documents verified and bank details confirmed by call-back, classified by the tree, carrying negotiated terms as templates and the supplier's true currency, paid on terms to verified accounts only, and kept true by the scheduled reads.</b> Module 2 covers items and buying costs.</p>"),
]

